**The honest dialectical pushback I'd hold.** Situatedness has a cost. Every turn that loads rich situated context is paying for that context in tokens, in Quick-slot latency, and in MoE coherence pressure. Not every turn benefits — "what's 2+2" doesn't need the user's project charter loaded. The naive interpretation of "situate everything more deeply" would make the system slower and possibly worse on trivial turns. The discipline the design doc implies but doesn't name is *adaptive situatedness*: the depth of situated context loaded should match the depth of the work, not be a constant. The routing layer is where this adaptive decision happens. So the answer to "where to do more situatedness" cuts both ways — yes, deepen it on the substantive surfaces (the trajectory engine, multi-actor mesh, corpora-as-biography, generative-bounds audit, the budget enforcer), but also explicitly *lighten* it on transactional turns so the cost-of-situatedness doesn't compound into latency that competes against frontier-model speed on simple queries. The situated agent principle is at its most powerful when the user notices it on the turns that mattered and didn't notice it on the turns that didn't.

